WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.142
When we treat health care as a retail commodity,

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.734
Getting sick drains a family's capital.

3
00:00:06.314 --> 00:00:10.374
True health system resilience treats care as economic protection.

4
00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.262
Take Bangladesh, for example.

5
00:00:12.714 --> 00:00:18.278
They are facing a massive surge in chronic conditions that require constant lifelong management.

6
00:00:18.634 --> 00:00:24.710
Non-communicable diseases like diabetes are now responsible for over 60% of all deaths.

7
00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:26.662
The public system can't keep up.

8
00:00:27.018 --> 00:00:31.206
With only 0.58 skilled medical staff per thousand people.

9
00:00:31.754 --> 00:00:35.782
This severe shortage forces the population to turn to the private sector

10
00:00:36.074 --> 00:00:38.950
that now holds 60% of total bed capacity.

11
00:00:39.466 --> 00:00:43.590
This leaves individuals paying for profit-driven treatments directly on their own.

12
00:00:44.042 --> 00:00:46.598
That mechanism is called out-of-pocket expenditure.

13
00:00:47.082 --> 00:00:51.462
In Bangladesh, over 68% of all health spending is funded this way.

14
00:00:51.754 --> 00:00:54.310
Draining personal finances for episodic care.

15
00:00:54.954 --> 00:01:00.838
For chronic illnesses, these uncoordinated, lifelong payments act exactly like a regressive tax.

16
00:01:01.162 --> 00:01:06.982
Instead of building economic resilience, this system systematically extracts wealth from the labor force.

17
00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:10.598
Because true health system resilience doesn't just treat the sick.

18
00:01:10.954 --> 00:01:13.990
It functions as the foundation of economic infrastructure.

19
00:01:14.474 --> 00:01:16.934
Human in purpose. Systemic in method.

20
00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.432
Protective in outcome.
